# Four of the six Docker labs were named by no workflow (2026-10-04)

`scripts/` ships six files ending in `-in-docker.sh`: five acceptance labs that build a real
binary or download a real artifact and interrogate it inside a container that has never seen
this repository, plus `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, the container entry point that owns the
gate list. On 2026-10-04 two of those six were named by a workflow. The other four were read
by nothing at all: their verdicts belonged to whoever happened to remember to run them.

This batch adds `scripts/ci/check-lab-coverage.py`, which asks of each of the six: does a
workflow name it on an executable line, or does it carry a dated row in
`scripts/ci/docker-labs.tsv` saying what CI cannot supply. It wires the two labs whose verdict
is about the commit under test into `.github/workflows/docker-labs.yml`, and re-runs the other
four so the ledger starts full and current rather than asserting a coverage nobody measured.

Everything quoted below is copied verbatim out of runs on 2026-10-04. The four lab re-runs are
recorded in the transcripts they belong to, each under a `## Re-run 2026-10-04` heading; this
file records the coverage rule around them.

