== 1. The drift, measured before anything was wired

Each lab script: the last commit that touched it, how far that commit sits behind `HEAD`, and
which workflow names it. `git grep` at `36d0809e` is the honest column, because the working
tree already contains the workflow this batch adds.

    $ for s in scripts/*-in-docker.sh; do
        last="$(git log -1 --format='%h %ad' --date=short -- "$s")"
        behind="$(git rev-list --count "$(git log -1 --format=%H -- "$s")..HEAD")"
        named="$(git grep -l -F "$s" -- '.github/workflows/*.yml' | paste -sd, -)"
        at_head="$(git grep -l -F "$s" 36d0809e -- '.github/workflows/*.yml' \
                   | sed 's/^[^:]*://' | paste -sd, -)"
        printf '%-42s %-16s %3s behind  now: %-34s then: %s\n' \
          "$s" "$last" "$behind" "${named:-nothing}" "${at_head:-nothing}"
      done
    scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh     9adaa44b 2026-10-02  50 behind  now: .github/workflows/ci.yml           then: .github/workflows/ci.yml
    scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh            9adaa44b 2026-10-02  50 behind  now: nothing                            then: nothing
    scripts/npm-install-in-docker.sh           9adaa44b 2026-10-02  50 behind  now: nothing                            then: nothing
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh     6013a862 2026-10-03  48 behind  now: .github/workflows/docker-labs.yml  then: nothing
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh                36d0809e 2026-10-04   0 behind  now: .github/workflows/ci.yml           then: .github/workflows/ci.yml
    scripts/web-deployment-in-docker.sh        0e89f3fc 2026-10-02  70 behind  now: .github/workflows/docker-labs.yml  then: nothing

At `36d0809e` four of the six were named by nothing, and the four newest transcripts were 48
to 70 commits behind the tree they claimed to describe. `check-guard-wiring.py` accounts for
`scripts/ci/`, one directory down, so a lab in `scripts/` is invisible to it: there was no
gate anywhere in the repository that could have noticed a lab going unrun.

The staleness is not cosmetic. `scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh` grew 28 checks in
`6013a862` on 2026-10-03 and its own transcript still showed the 102-check run; the 130-check
transcript landed in `docs/verification/remote-provenance-linux-2026-10-02.log` instead. That
is written up in `docs/verification/remote-acceptance-linux-2026-10-02.log`.

== 2. The rule, and the reason it is two rules

A lab goes unrun in two different ways and they need opposite answers.

A lab whose verdict is about the commit under test should simply be run by CI. The gate
measures "can be run by CI" rather than believing it: the script has to appear on an
executable line of some `.github/workflows/*.yml`. Whole-line comments and trailing comments
do not count, so a workflow that mentions a lab while explaining why it is not wired fails the
gate; both shapes are fixtures (`test_a_comment_mentioning_the_lab_is_not_an_invocation`,
`test_a_commented_out_command_at_end_of_a_line_is_not_an_invocation`).

A lab whose verdict is about something other than the commit cannot be a merge gate at all.
`npm-install-in-docker.sh` asks what the public registry serves today;
`install-sh-in-docker.sh` installs a published release artifact, not the tree being merged.
Those get a row in `scripts/ci/docker-labs.tsv` with a category, the transcript the run
produced, the date and commit it ran at, and the thing CI cannot supply. The row is not an
exemption from being run: its date has to be within 30 days or the gate goes red and names the
command to re-run. A documented-but-never-run lab therefore fails on a schedule instead of
becoming archaeology.

Which is the whole point of the ledger being a *dated claim*. An allow list says "this one is
allowed to be unrun, forever". This says "somebody saw this pass, or saw exactly why it cannot
pass, on this day, at this commit" -- and goes red when that stops being true.

== 3. The ledger and the classification, as they stand

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-lab-coverage.py --list
    scripts/install-integrity-in-docker.sh	run by CI	.github/workflows/ci.yml
    scripts/install-sh-in-docker.sh	ledgered	2026-10-04	36d0809e	green	consumes-published-artifact
    scripts/npm-install-in-docker.sh	ledgered	2026-10-04	36d0809e	red	consumes-published-artifact
    scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh	run by CI	.github/workflows/docker-labs.yml
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh	ledgered	2026-10-04	a04e91a7	green	already-done-in-ci
    scripts/web-deployment-in-docker.sh	run by CI	.github/workflows/docker-labs.yml

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-lab-coverage.py
    check-lab-coverage: OK (6 lab(s): 3 run by CI, 3 with a current ledger row)

Two of the three ledger rows are `green` and one is `red`. A red row is not a failure of the
gate and the gate does not treat it as one: the ledger records what the last run said, and the
verdict field exists so that "we know this one is red, and why" is written down instead of
being re-litigated by the next person to run the lab.

The `verify-in-docker` row is the one a reader should be suspicious of, because the container
entry point is what CI itself runs on a push. Its reason says what is actually true: the
`rust`, `clippy` and `script-job` steps run the same command list natively on every push, so a
container leg in CI would measure the image build and the volume caches rather than the
change, and the sweep inside the image runs to about two hours. The row is dated `a04e91a7`,
the last commit it was actually run at, which is a different commit from the other two rows.

== 4. The two labs that became CI's

`.github/workflows/docker-labs.yml` is new. It is not wired into `ci.yml`: each of the two
labs pays for a cold `cargo` build plus a container image build, so a per-push leg would cost
more than the two verdicts are worth on every commit. It runs on `cron: "17 3 * * *"` plus
`workflow_dispatch`, so a lab you are changing can be watched on demand.

    name: Docker acceptance labs

    on:
      schedule:
        - cron: "17 3 * * *"
      workflow_dispatch:
    ...
          - name: Web host behind a TLS-terminating proxy
            shell: bash
            run: bash scripts/web-deployment-in-docker.sh

          - name: M4 remote workspace acceptance
            shell: bash
            run: bash scripts/remote-acceptance-in-docker.sh

`timeout-minutes: 150` is a budget, not a measurement: neither lab has been timed on a GitHub
runner, and the comment in the file says so. It is stated high because a scheduled job that
hits a low ceiling reports a timeout instead of a verdict, and nobody is watching at 03:17
UTC.

Two dependencies had to be stated rather than assumed present, and
`check-workflow-toolchain.py` is what forced them into the open. `socat` is the tunnel between
the two machines in the remote lab -- the transport dials loopback only, so killing `socat` is
how a dropped network is produced -- and `openssl` mints the lab CA the TLS lab hands to
`curl`. Neither is on `ubuntu-latest` by the lab's own account; the lab refuses to run without
them, so the job installs them. `nginx:1.27-alpine` is pulled explicitly for the same reason:
the TLS lab refuses to pull mid-check.

Making the third workflow file visible required changing the three workflow gates. All three
had `["ci.yml", "release.yml"]` written out, so a new file would have been checked by nothing,
including by the gates whose whole job is checking workflow files. Each now globs
`.github/workflows/*.yml` and refuses to pass on an empty result:

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-workflow-yaml.py
    workflow yaml: 3 file(s), no unquoted scalar with a mapping separator

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-workflow-shells.py
    check-workflow-shells: .github/workflows/ci.yml: OK
    check-workflow-shells: .github/workflows/docker-labs.yml: OK
    check-workflow-shells: .github/workflows/release.yml: OK

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-workflow-toolchain.py
    check-workflow-toolchain: OK (3 workflow file(s), 2 tool rule(s))

`check-workflow-shells.py` gained one more fix while it was being opened: it recognised a
matrix `os:` entry only in flow form. A block-form entry is the same claim about the same
runner and was passing unchecked.

