== 6. The npm lab is red for a reason CI cannot fix

    FAILED checks:
      - pinned but unpublished platform packages (npm installs these silently):
         - chaos-code-win32-arm64@0.2.110 (published: 0.0.1-security)
         - chaos-code-win32-x64@0.2.110 (published: 0.0.1-security)
    13 check(s) run, at least one failed

The check reads the `optionalDependencies` of the launcher package the registry actually
served, installed inside the container, and asks of each pin whether that version exists under
that name. The registry has `0.0.1-security` and nothing else for both `win32` names -- that
is the version npm itself publishes when a name is reserved as a security placeholder. `npm`
treats a missing optional dependency as normal, so a Windows user gets the launcher and no
binary; the launcher's own error is the only sign, and the lab asserts that error is loud:

    ok  with no win32 binary present the launcher fails loudly (exit 1): chaos-code: no platform binary installed for win32-x64.

The fix is to publish two packages. That is a release action, and this repository does not
perform releases from CI, which is exactly what the ledger row's reason field is for.

The same comparison done against the launcher manifest in the tree, rather than the one the
registry serves, shows the wider shape of it:

    $ python3 - <<'PY'
    import json, subprocess
    d = json.load(open("crates/codegen/xai-grok-pager/npm/chaos/package.json"))
    print("launcher package.json version:", d["version"])
    for k, v in (d.get("optionalDependencies") or {}).items():
        out = subprocess.run(["npm", "view", k, "versions", "--json"],
                             capture_output=True, text=True).stdout
        have = "".join(out.split()).replace('""', ",").strip("[]").strip('"')
        mark = "yes" if v in have.split(",") else "NO"
        print(f"  {k:26} pinned {v}  on registry: {have}  pinned version exists: {mark}")
    PY
    launcher package.json version: 0.4.2
      chaos-code-darwin-arm64    pinned 0.4.2  on registry: 0.2.110  pinned version exists: NO
      chaos-code-darwin-x64      pinned 0.4.2  on registry: 0.2.110  pinned version exists: NO
      chaos-code-linux-arm64     pinned 0.4.2  on registry: 0.2.110  pinned version exists: NO
      chaos-code-linux-x64       pinned 0.4.2  on registry: 0.2.110  pinned version exists: NO
      chaos-code-win32-arm64     pinned 0.4.2  on registry: 0.0.1-security  pinned version exists: NO
      chaos-code-win32-x64       pinned 0.4.2  on registry: 0.0.1-security  pinned version exists: NO

The four `NO`s that are not the lab's failures are the ordinary case: the tree is at `0.4.2`
and `0.4.2` has not been published yet, so no platform package carries it. Reading this block
without that distinction is how a registry-state problem gets mistaken for a build problem, so
the lab's wording names the specific pins rather than counting mismatches.

== 7. A green lab total that hides which host served the bytes

The `install-sh` re-run passed 19 of 19 and printed this while doing it:

    |   origin:  https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/download/v0.4.2/chaos-linux-x64
    |   mirror:  origin first, then public fallbacks
    | downloading...
    |   try: https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/download/v0.4.2/chaos-linux-x64
    |   try: https://ghfast.top/https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/download/v0.4.2/chaos-linux-x64
    |   from: https://ghfast.top/https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/download/v0.4.2/chaos-linux-x64

`try:` origin, then `try:` the mirror, then `from:` the mirror. The install was served by a
public third-party mirror, and the run that called itself green had to be read line by line to
notice. Two source-level facts explain why, both re-read on 2026-10-04:

    $ grep -n 'download_github "$ORIGIN_URL"' scripts/install.sh
    530:USED_URL="$(download_github "$ORIGIN_URL" "$TMP" 12 0 1048576)" || exit 1

The fourth field is `max_time`, and `download_github` only appends `--max-time` when it is
positive:

    $ grep -n -- '--max-time\|--speed-limit\|--speed-time\|--limit-rate' scripts/install.sh
    295:    curl_args+=(--max-time "$max_time")
    352:    if curl -fsSL --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 30 -o "$tmp" "$cand" 2>/dev/null; then
    367:      curl -fsSLI --connect-timeout 10 --max-time 30 "$cand" 2>/dev/null \

So the big fetch has a 12-second connect timeout and no ceiling on the transfer itself, and no
minimum throughput anywhere in the script. That is deliberate -- the comment at `:528` says a
140 MB+ asset must not be cut off mid-body -- but the artifact's real size is worth putting
next to it:

    $ curl -sIL -o /dev/null -D - \
        https://github.com/chao2hang/chaos-code/releases/download/v0.4.2/chaos-linux-x64 \
        | grep -i '^content-length' | tail -1
    content-length: 161233728

153 MiB with no wall-clock ceiling and no `--speed-limit` means a mirror that trickles the
body at any rate above zero holds the install open indefinitely. The `min_bytes` floor of
1048576 catches a truncated or proxy-substituted body, which is the defect it was written for,
but it can only be evaluated once the transfer ends.

The other half: `download_github` builds a `reasons` list as it walks candidates and prints it
only on the failure path -- the `printf '%s' "$reasons" | awk 'NF && !seen[$0]++'` followed by
`head -4` at `scripts/install.sh:339`. On success it prints the winning URL and returns. A
user -- or a lab -- therefore cannot tell whether origin answered 404, answered with a body
`min_bytes` rejected, or was never reachable, which is precisely the question to ask of an
install whose bytes came from a mirror. Both halves are filed as TODO rows; neither is
silently fixed here, because changing the installer's failure semantics needs its own evidence
and its own fixtures.

== 8. The gate is not vacuous: 39 mutants, 0 survived

Each mutation is a source edit to the shipped file, applied to a scratch copy, followed by the
real gate plus its real fixtures; a mutant survives if the suite still passes. Every driver
checks its baseline first and aborts rather than reporting kills against a red baseline, and
each restores its subject with plain `cp` verified by `cmp`. The five specs mutate, in order:
`scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`, `scripts/ci/check-lab-coverage.py`,
`scripts/ci/check-workflow-shells.py`, `scripts/ci/check-workflow-yaml.py` and
`scripts/ci/check-workflow-toolchain.py`. The run in full, including which fixture killed each
mutant -- an unmatched anchor is reported as SURVIVED, and a fixture failing that was not
expected to is named under `also failed (unexpected)`:

